## The Week at a Glance

- 🔴 **MacSync Stealer is actively targeting Mac users and rotating infrastructure to avoid detection:** This credential-stealing malware targets macOS devices, harvests browser-stored passwords and session tokens, and cycles through new domains fast enough to evade traditional blocklists, putting any employee on an unmanaged or lightly managed Mac at risk of account takeover.
- 🟡 **Outlook DLP enforcement on Mac and mobile arrives in September:** Organizations that rely on Data Loss Prevention policies to meet compliance obligations should confirm their policies will apply correctly to Mac and mobile users before the rollout date, or face a coverage gap.
- 🟡 **Exchange Web Services retirement begins in October:** A protocol that many third-party tools, line-of-business integrations, and calendar-sharing configurations depend on will start going offline in October 2026, with full shutdown in April 2027. Organizations with on-premises Exchange or hybrid environments need to assess exposure now.
- 🟢 **Entra custom sign-in page CSS begins retiring October 26:** Organizations with custom-branded login pages must plan a visual refresh before that date to maintain a consistent employee and partner sign-in experience.

*Sources: [Microsoft Security Blog](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/08/18/hunting-macsync-stealer-infrastructure-through-behavioral-pivots/) · [M365 Roadmap - DLP Mac](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569719) · [Exchange Team Blog - EWS Retirement](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/exchange-team-blog/help-us-shape-exchange-server-on-premises-to-different-online/ba-p/4549691) · [Entra ID Blog](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-entra-blog/microsoft-entra-id-enhances-security-of-branded-sign-ins/ba-p/4537471)*

---

## Why This Week Matters

Mac and mobile endpoints have historically been treated as lower-risk than Windows devices, but credential-stealing malware and a DLP gap on those platforms together expose a meaningful blind spot in most organizations' security and compliance programs. At the same time, the Exchange Web Services retirement clock is running: any business application or calendar integration that has not been assessed for EWS dependency faces a hard outage in under eight months. Leadership should treat this week as a prompt to confirm that endpoint coverage, compliance policy scope, and integration inventories are not lagging behind the threat and deprecation timelines.

*Sources: [Microsoft Security Blog - MacSync Stealer](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/08/18/hunting-macsync-stealer-infrastructure-through-behavioral-pivots/) · [Exchange Team Blog - EWS Retirement](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/exchange-team-blog/help-us-shape-exchange-server-on-premises-to-different-online/ba-p/4549691)*

---

## Risk & Compliance

- 🔴 **[MacSync Stealer targeting Mac endpoints](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/08/18/hunting-macsync-stealer-infrastructure-through-behavioral-pivots/):** This active credential-stealing campaign harvests browser passwords and session tokens from macOS devices, enabling attackers to bypass multi-factor authentication and take over accounts; organizations in regulated industries (HIPAA, SOC 2, CMMC) face heightened breach-notification and access-control obligations if employee credentials are compromised, and security teams should validate Mac endpoint coverage immediately. This gap in endpoint verification represents an implicit-trust vulnerability that a mature Zero Trust posture would close by requiring continuous device health signals before granting access.

- 🟡 **[Outlook DLP Wait-on-Send for Mac - September GA](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569719):** Until this feature is live, Mac users in Outlook can send emails containing sensitive data without triggering the same pre-send policy checks that Windows users encounter; organizations subject to HIPAA, GDPR, or state privacy laws should confirm enforcement scope and communicate the change to compliance and legal teams before September.

- 🟡 **[Outlook DLP Wait-on-Send for iOS and Android - September GA](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569718):** The same pre-send DLP gap exists for mobile Outlook users today; HIPAA- and GDPR-covered organizations should treat this September launch as a compliance milestone and verify that mobile device management policies are aligned so enforcement kicks in automatically on rollout.

- 🟡 **[Purview DLP for Calendar Events on Outlook Mobile - September GA](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569716):** Meeting invitations sent from iOS and Android can currently carry sensitive information in subjects and descriptions without triggering DLP controls; this gap is particularly relevant for organizations in healthcare and financial services, where meeting content regularly includes regulated data, and the September GA date is the target for full coverage.

- 🟡 **[Exchange Web Services retirement begins October 2026](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/exchange-team-blog/help-us-shape-exchange-server-on-premises-to-different-online/ba-p/4549691):** Third-party apps, calendar integrations, and hybrid Free/Busy configurations that rely on EWS in Exchange Online will start failing as Microsoft phases out the protocol starting October 2026, with full shutdown in April 2027; IT and procurement teams need an inventory of EWS-dependent tools within the next 30 days to avoid service disruptions.

- 🟡 **[Entra ID custom sign-in CSS retirement - October 26, 2026](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-entra-blog/microsoft-entra-id-enhances-security-of-branded-sign-ins/ba-p/4537471):** Custom CSS layout and positioning properties used to brand the employee and partner sign-in page will stop working on October 26; organizations with heavily customized login pages risk a broken or unstyled sign-in experience for employees, partners, and customers if no action is taken before that date. This change is part of Microsoft's Secure Future Initiative and reduces the attack surface that custom CSS injection could expose, strengthening sign-in page integrity.

- 🟡 **[Purview Data Security Investigations from endpoint DLP alerts - September GA](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=558547):** A new workflow lets compliance and security teams jump directly from a DLP alert into an investigation of the files involved, shortening the time to assess a potential data exfiltration event; organizations under SOC 2 or HIPAA should factor this capability into their incident-response procedures before the September launch.

- 🟢 **[Purview Retention for Copilot Memory - September GA](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569612):** Copilot's personalized memory items, stored in user mailboxes, will be subject to retention and versioning policies; organizations with records-management obligations should confirm that existing retention policies appropriately cover this new data class before the feature is broadly available.

- 🟢 **[CVE-2026-62834 - Azure Data Factory Elevation of Privilege](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-62834):** An improper cryptographic signature check in Azure Data Factory could allow an attacker to escalate privileges over the network; organizations using Azure Data Factory in data pipelines should confirm their environments are patched and verify that least-privilege access controls are in place.

- 🟢 **[CVE-2026-50661 - Windows BitLocker Security Feature Bypass](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-50661):** An updated acknowledgment was published for this BitLocker bypass vulnerability; organizations relying on BitLocker as a primary encryption control for compliance (CMMC, HIPAA) should confirm that endpoint patching is current.

*Sources: [Microsoft Security Blog - MacSync Stealer](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/08/18/hunting-macsync-stealer-infrastructure-through-behavioral-pivots/) · [Microsoft Security Response Center](https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-62834) · [M365 Roadmap](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569719)*

