## What Your Employees Will Notice

- **Outlook on Mac and mobile will pause briefly before sending certain emails starting in September.** When a message triggers a DLP policy, Outlook will hold the send action for a configurable period while the policy is evaluated. Employees may see a short delay or a policy tip asking them to review the content. Proactive communication will reduce help desk calls.

- **Outlook calendar invites on mobile may show policy tips starting in September.** Employees who include sensitive details in meeting subjects or descriptions may see a new notification on iOS and Android. This is expected behavior, not an error.

- **The company sign-in page may look slightly different after October 26.** If your organization uses a custom-branded login page, IT teams will need to update the design before that date. Employees who notice a visual change should be reassured it is intentional.

- **Planner now supports tabs in private and shared Teams channels.** Teams that have been waiting to manage tasks inside private or shared channels can now add Planner directly as a tab, without leaving Teams.

- **Inbox rule management in Outlook for Windows is getting a bulk-action upgrade in November.** Employees who maintain many inbox rules will be able to enable, disable, or delete multiple rules at once, saving time during cleanup.

- **GitHub Copilot can now be @mentioned directly in Teams conversations.** Developer teams can hand off a discussion thread directly to GitHub Copilot to generate code without switching tools, which may prompt questions from non-developer staff about what the bot is doing in their channels.

*Sources: [M365 Roadmap - DLP Mac](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569719) · [M365 Roadmap - Planner Private Channels](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=558928) · [Teams Blog - GitHub Copilot in Teams](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-teams-blog/turn-conversations-into-code-with-github-copilot-in-microsoft/ba-p/4548305)*

---

## What Your Help Desk Should Expect

- **"Why is my email not sending?" calls from Mac and mobile users starting in September.** The DLP wait-on-send feature will be new to these users. Pre-launch communication and a short FAQ will reduce ticket volume significantly.

- **Confusion about policy tips on mobile calendar invites.** Users who receive a DLP notification when creating a meeting may not understand why. Help desk staff should be briefed on the new calendar DLP feature before September.

- **Sign-in page appearance questions after October 26.** If the branded sign-in page changes, employees and partners may contact IT assuming something is broken. A brief announcement ahead of the change will preempt most of these contacts.

- **Questions about slow or failed third-party integrations beginning in October.** As EWS deprecation begins, tools that have not been updated may start behaving erratically. Help desk teams should have an escalation path to IT operations for EWS-related failures.

- **Planner tab setup requests for private and shared Teams channels.** Now that Planner is available in private and shared channels, teams may ask IT to help configure it. This is self-service but may generate tickets from less-technical users.

*Sources: [M365 Roadmap - DLP Mac](https://www.microsoft.com/microsoft-365/roadmap?id=569719) · [Exchange Team Blog - EWS Retirement](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/exchange-team-blog/help-us-shape-exchange-server-on-premises-to-different-online/ba-p/4549691) · [Entra ID Blog](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/microsoft-entra-blog/microsoft-entra-id-enhances-security-of-branded-sign-ins/ba-p/4537471)*

